    ☐ Keep RangeSlider, Slider, and DimensionsTooltip lower priority for the DwarfSpec runner while retaining them in the DFHack library-completeness backlog.

1.7 Official DFHack widget coverage gate:
    ☐ Complete all 20 missing standalone widget mockups before introducing an equivalent custom primitive.
    ☐ Verify that BannerPanel has a standalone specimen even though it is implicit in TextButton.
    ☐ Verify that every official widget page uses font.baseGame, the single game text size, linked palette presets, and existing CP437 assets.
    ☐ Verify that every official widget specimen is wrapped in an appropriately named top-level group or board.
    ☐ Verify that every official widget page uses the established dark Canvas Background.
    ☐ Record why an official widget is unsuitable wherever a later task retains a custom alternative.

1.8 Component architecture rules:
    ☐ Organize the proposal into official DFHack widgets, custom generic components, DwarfSpec domain components, reusable screen panels, and workspace mockups.
    ☐ Give every component one clearly documented responsibility.
    ☐ Document what each complex component owns and explicitly does not own.
    ☐ Extract repeated structures instead of duplicating their shapes across components or mockup states.
    ☐ Compose official DFHack widgets and existing primitives inside domain components instead of copying their borders, labels, scrollbars, or interaction states.
    ☐ Keep cross-panel selection and synchronization in the workspace/controller layer rather than individual panels.
    ☐ Keep status calculation, aggregation, flake detection, sorting, capture, and editor launching outside presentation-only components.
    ☐ Avoid one component with a Cartesian product of unrelated variant dimensions.
    ☐ Prefer nested state components and content overrides over separate copies of an entire complex component.
    ☐ Do not create components for one-off headings, dividers, explanatory text, individual footer counts, stack separators, or complete workspace mockups.
    ☐ Create a component when an element is reused, has independent state or interaction, contains repeated children, owns scrolling or clipping, or appears across multiple mockup states.

1.9 Product and data ownership:
    ☐ Document the four selection levels: run, file or suite, test attempt, and command or event step.
    ☐ Define which selection level owns each action, status, summary, log, and evidence view.
    ☐ Define the minimum information required to answer: what failed, why, at which step, in which attempt, and what should be rerun.
    ☐ Define the boundary between the structured command trace and raw DFHack output.
    ☐ Define the boundary between DwarfSpec's Debug action and external VS Code debugging.
    ☐ Define a view-model contract for run state, progress, counts, scope, elapsed time, and reproduce command.
    ☐ Define a view-model contract for explorer nodes and their supplied aggregate statuses.
    ☐ Define a view-model contract for test results, attempts, failure category, behavior outcome, cleanup outcome, and infrastructure outcome.
    ☐ Define a view-model contract for trace steps, groups, durations, retry or wait data, source locations, evidence references, and raw-log anchors.
    ☐ Define a view-model contract for captured or textual host evidence and its availability state.
    ☐ Keep TEST RESULTS as the pane title; use the breadcrumb to identify its current scope.
    ☐ Show the selected test name and active attempt without overloading the breadcrumb.

1.10 Runtime capability decisions:
    ☐ Confirm which run modes can be configured.
    ☐ Confirm whether repeat count is configurable from the UI.
    ☐ Confirm whether the runner can report attempt-level outcomes.
    ☐ Confirm whether the runner can identify flaky outcomes within one repeated run.
    ☐ Confirm whether the runner can emit structured command and event steps.
    ☐ Confirm whether trace steps can identify source locations and raw-log anchors.
    ☐ Confirm whether the runner can provide retry, wait, or polling details.
    ☐ Confirm which host-evidence modes can be provided.
    ☐ Confirm whether evidence is live, captured, retained, inferred, or unavailable.
    ☐ Confirm whether Open Source can target file, line, and column in the configured editor.
    ☐ Confirm which debug-break and pause options are supported.
    ☐ Confirm whether Host Events can be separated reliably from raw DFHack output.
    ☐ Remove or mark unavailable every affordance not supported by the confirmed contracts.

1.11 Current proposal cleanup:
    ☐ Remove the TEST, RESULT, and R headers from Tree View With Columns.
    ☐ Remove the duplicate Result Column Indicator list from the test explorer.
    ☐ Keep exactly one status indicator inside each tree row and inside the tree's shared scroll panel.
    ☐ Replace every typed visual symbol with the corresponding existing CP437 tile asset.
        ☐ Replace typed connection, follow-output, and host-responsive bullets.
        ☐ Replace typed pass, failure, and error marks.
        ☐ Replace typed arrows in stacks, flows, and event descriptions.
        ☐ Replace typed multiplication and separator symbols where they serve as visual symbols.
    ☐ Remove or archive the hidden list-based result layers superseded by Generic Table View.
    ☐ Remove or archive other hidden, superseded presentation layers after verifying that no live component instance depends on them.
    ☐ Remove the decorative RUN / INSPECT / REPRODUCE / RESTORE title-bar copy.
    ☐ Remove any protocol-version or DFHack-version badges.
    ☐ Consolidate the duplicate top command preview and bottom rerun command.

1.12 Existing DFHack component reuse:
    ☐ Reuse Panel for framed or grouped screen regions.
    ☐ Reuse Window for the top-level runner window.
    ☐ Reuse ResizingPanel for content-sized groups when automatic sizing matches the layout.
    ☐ Reuse Pages wherever exactly one of several child views is visible.
    ☐ Reuse Divider instead of drawing one-off separator rectangles.
    ☐ Reuse TextButton for clickable actions.
    ☐ Reuse ConfigureButton for compact configuration launchers.
    ☐ Reuse HelpButton for command-help launchers.
    ☐ Reuse RadioButton for compact graphical binary choices.
    ☐ Reuse HotkeyLabel only for genuine clickable hotkey labels.
    ☐ Reuse CycleHotkeyLabel for small finite choices that can be cycled without opening a menu.
    ☐ Reuse ToggleHotkeyLabel for labeled textual On and Off choices.
    ☐ Reuse ButtonGroup when synchronized graphical choices and a CycleHotkeyLabel are both required.
    ☐ Reuse Dropdown Control only when exposing the complete choice set in a popup is more usable than CycleHotkeyLabel.
    ☐ Reuse EditField only for single-line editable or copyable field interactions.
    ☐ Reuse TextArea only for genuinely multiline editable text.
    ☐ Reuse WrappedLabel for responsive multiline read-only text.
    ☐ Reuse TooltipLabel for dynamically visible tooltip or contextual help text.
    ☐ Reuse Breadcrumb in the Test Results title bar.
    ☐ Reuse Tree View With Columns as the test-explorer container.
    ☐ Reuse Generic Table View as the test-result index.
    ☐ Reuse the normal Scrollbar wherever scrolling is required.
    ☐ Reuse widgets.List and its item presentation for chronological or selectable lists when their semantics match.
    ☐ Reuse FilteredList for flat filterable lists instead of rebuilding List, EditField, and no-match Label composition.
    ☐ Reuse Tab and TabBar for tab navigation.
    ☐ Reuse Pages as the content container paired with TabBar.
    ☐ Reuse Slider and RangeSlider when their value-selection semantics match.
    ☐ Reuse DimensionsTooltip only for cursor-following 3D dimension feedback.
    ☐ Reuse Command Menu only for transient action menus.
    ☐ Reuse issue for concise failure or unavailable-state messaging when its semantics match.
    ☐ Reuse targeting_prompt only for an active targeting or armed-debug prompt.
    ☐ Record why any new component cannot be represented by an existing component or a new variant of one.

Phase 2: Build the generic component system
    ☐ Exit with linked, DwarfSpec-independent generic components whose responsibilities, reuse points, and dependency direction are documented and verified.

2.1 Generic component: Status Indicator:
    ☐ Create a Status Indicator component that owns only a CP437 status asset and palette-linked color.
    ☐ Define queued, running, passed, failed, errored, skipped, cancelled, not-run, flaky, cleanup-failed, and host-disconnected statuses.
    ☐ Assign every status an existing CP437 asset.
    ☐ Assign every status an established DF palette preset.
    ☐ Do not make Status Indicator calculate aggregate status or cleanup precedence.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Status Indicator and its variants.

2.2 Generic component: Status Badge:
    ☐ Create a Status Badge component that composes Status Indicator with a short text label and optional border.
    ☐ Define compact, bordered, and unbordered presentations only where each has a demonstrated reuse.
    ☐ Pair symbol, color, and text so status meaning never depends on color alone.
    ☐ Use Status Badge for connection, host responsiveness, dirty-host, and similarly compact status summaries.
    ☐ Do not encode DwarfSpec aggregation rules inside Status Badge.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Status Badge and its variants.

2.3 Generic component: Tree Row With Columns:
    ☐ Extract Tree Row With Columns from the existing Tree View With Columns master.
    ☐ Make the row own its expander slot, label slot, arbitrary column slots, selection state, and integrated right-side status slot.
    ☐ Keep all row content inside the tree's shared scroll viewport.
    ☐ Define leaf, collapsed-parent, expanded-parent, selected, focused, and disabled row presentations.
    ☐ Use existing CP437 expander assets.
    ☐ Do not add column headers to Tree Row With Columns or Tree View With Columns.
    ☐ Refactor Tree View With Columns to repeat component-linked Tree Row With Columns instances.
    ☐ Verify that refactoring the master preserves all existing linked instances.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Tree Row With Columns and its variants.

2.4 Generic components: Table Cell, Table Row, and Sortable Header Cell:
    ☐ Extract Table Cell from the existing Generic Table View master.
    ☐ Make Table Cell own standard padding, clipping, alignment, and text overflow.
    ☐ Extract Table Row as an arbitrary collection of Table Cell instances.
    ☐ Define normal, selected, focused, disabled, and alternate-row presentations only if the table uses them.
    ☐ Extract Sortable Header Cell from the existing Generic Table View master.
    ☐ Define none, ascending, and descending sort presentations.
    ☐ Use existing CP437 up and down arrow assets for sort indicators.
    ☐ Keep header styling within the black, white, and brown palette.
    ☐ Keep default none sorting equivalent to the original order supplied by the view-model.
    ☐ Refactor Generic Table View to compose Sortable Header Cell, Table Row, Table Cell, and the normal Scrollbar.
    ☐ Keep Generic Table View configurable for arbitrary columns and rows.
    ☐ Do not encode DwarfSpec column names, statuses, or ordering rules in Generic Table View.
    ☐ Verify that refactoring the master preserves all existing linked instances.
    ☐ Create dedicated component pages for Table Cell, Table Row, and Sortable Header Cell.

2.5 Generic components: Dropdown Field, Menu Panel, and Menu Item:
    ☐ Retain the existing closed and open Dropdown Control components.
    ☐ Document why Dropdown Control is required for a use case instead of CycleHotkeyLabel.
    ☐ Extract or formalize the shared Dropdown Field used by both closed and open states.
    ☐ Keep the static label, selected value, and CP437 downward-triangle button inside Dropdown Field.
    ☐ Keep the triangle button free of surrounding brackets.
    ☐ Show the triangle in the established normal color when closed and orange when open.
    ☐ Extract Dropdown Menu Panel with the same border treatment as Dropdown Field.
    ☐ Extract Dropdown Menu Item for normal, focused, selected, and disabled states.
    ☐ Show a selected menu item with cyan text on a black background.
    ☐ Refactor Dropdown Control Open to compose Dropdown Field, Dropdown Menu Panel, and repeated Dropdown Menu Item instances.
    ☐ Verify that refactoring the masters preserves all existing linked instances.
    ☐ Create dedicated component pages for Dropdown Field, Dropdown Menu Panel, and Dropdown Menu Item.

2.6 Official DFHack widgets: Tab, TabBar, and Pages application:
    ☐ Reuse TabBar for raw-output and diagnostic navigation.
    ☐ Reuse component-linked Tab instances inside TabBar.
    ☐ Reuse Pages for the content views selected by TabBar.
    ☐ Keep tab content and selected-page state supplied by the parent view-model.
    ☐ Do not use TabBar for chronological trace-step selection.
    ☐ Do not create a parallel custom Tab or Tab Bar component.

2.7 Official DFHack widgets: binary-choice application:
    ☐ Reuse ToggleHotkeyLabel for a labeled textual On or Off setting.
    ☐ Reuse RadioButton for a compact graphical checkbox or radio setting.
    ☐ Reuse ButtonGroup for synchronized graphical option selection only when its full behavior is required.
    ☐ Use existing CP437 assets and established palette presets in every choice widget.
    ☐ Reuse ToggleHotkeyLabel or RadioButton for Follow Output, Wrap, and supported debug options.
    ☐ Do not create a parallel custom Toggle component.

2.8 Generic component: Source Reference:
    ☐ Create a Source Reference component for file, line, and optional column.
    ☐ Define compact-inline and full-path presentations only if both are used.
    ☐ Compose an existing TextButton or equivalent existing action treatment for Open Source.
    ☐ Keep source display separate from editor-launch behavior.
    ☐ Reuse Source Reference in result details, trace steps, code frames, and stack entries.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Source Reference and its variants.

2.9 Generic components: Property Row and Property List:
    ☐ Create Property Row for one label and value pair.
    ☐ Define value states for normal, warning, failure, unavailable, and actionable only where required.
    ☐ Create Property List as a Panel or ResizingPanel containing repeated Property Row instances with consistent label alignment.
    ☐ Reuse Property List for host metadata, selected-step details, and evidence metadata.
    ☐ Keep Property List free of host-specific field names.
    ☐ Create dedicated component pages for Property Row and Property List.

2.10 Generic component: Log Row:
    ☐ Create Log Row with timestamp, source, severity or status, and Label or WrappedLabel message slots.
    ☐ Define normal, selected, correlated, warning, failure, and wrapped presentations.
    ☐ Compose Status Indicator where severity needs a visual indicator.
    ☐ Keep raw-log filtering, buffering, and truncation outside Log Row.
    ☐ Reuse Log Row for DFHack output and supported host-event streams.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Log Row and its variants.

Phase 3: Build the DwarfSpec domain component system
    ☐ Exit with linked DwarfSpec domain components that compose official and generic components without absorbing runner orchestration or runtime behavior.

3.1 DwarfSpec component: Attempt Indicator:
    ☐ Create Attempt Indicator from an attempt number or label plus Status Indicator.
    ☐ Define normal, selected, focused, and disabled states without duplicating every possible status as parent-level artwork.
    ☐ Keep flake calculation outside Attempt Indicator.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Attempt Indicator and its variants.

3.2 DwarfSpec component: Attempt Selector:
    ☐ Create Attempt Selector as a collection of Attempt Indicator instances plus current-attempt and total-attempt context.
    ☐ Evaluate ButtonGroup as the selection container before retaining the custom Attempt Selector layout.
    ☐ Retain the custom Attempt Selector only if ButtonGroup cannot represent per-attempt status and overflow requirements.
    ☐ Show a compact status for every supplied attempt.
    ☐ Define the no-repeat and single-attempt presentation without reserving dead space.
    ☐ Define overflow behavior for many attempts.
    ☐ Keep attempt selection outside the component's presentation contract.
    ☐ Keep FLAKY defined by supplied inconsistent attempt outcomes unless historical data is explicitly available.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Attempt Selector and its states.

3.3 DwarfSpec components: Trace Step Row and Trace Group Row:
    ☐ Define the ordered step taxonomy for setup, test commands, inputs, waits, assertions, cleanup, host events, and runner events.
    ☐ Create Trace Step Row from Status Indicator, optional Source Reference, and supplied step content.
    ☐ Show sequence, category, command or event, important target or argument, duration, and optional retry or wait information.
    ☐ Define queued, running, passed, failed, errored, skipped, cancelled, and not-run step states.
    ☐ Define selected, focused, expanded, and collapsed structural states without duplicating status artwork.
    ☐ Show retry, poll, or wait information only when supplied by the runner.
    ☐ Show a source reference only when one exists.
    ☐ Create Trace Group Row for setup, behavior, and cleanup grouping.
    ☐ Compose Status Indicator and existing CP437 expander assets in Trace Group Row.
    ☐ Show aggregate group status, duration, and child count.
    ☐ Keep group expansion from changing chronological ordering.
    ☐ Keep status aggregation outside Trace Group Row.
    ☐ Create dedicated component pages for Trace Step Row and Trace Group Row.

3.4 DwarfSpec component: Command Trace:
    ☐ Create Command Trace from widgets.List, Trace Group Row, Trace Step Row, and the normal Scrollbar.
    ☐ Keep the chronological trace as the primary selected-attempt debugging view.
    ☐ Define empty, loading, streaming, complete, failed, filtered-empty, and unavailable states.
    ☐ Define long-command, long-argument, nested-command, and large-trace scrolling behavior.
    ☐ Keep the selected failing step visible when changing attempts or related log views.
    ☐ Define behavior when a step has no host evidence or raw-log anchor.
    ☐ Keep failure formatting, host capture, and raw-output rendering outside Command Trace.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Command Trace and its states.

3.5 DwarfSpec components: Evidence Value Block and Failure Comparison:
    ☐ Create Evidence Value Block from a label and WrappedLabel for one labeled, multiline evidence value.
    ☐ Define normal, expected, actual, warning, failure, and unavailable presentations only where required.
    ☐ Create Failure Comparison from two Evidence Value Block instances.
    ☐ Support expected versus actual, requested versus observed, and previous versus current labels without new component copies.
    ☐ Keep comparison calculation and diff generation outside Failure Comparison.
    ☐ Define empty-side, long-value, and multiline overflow behavior.
    ☐ Create dedicated component pages for Evidence Value Block and Failure Comparison.

3.6 DwarfSpec component: Host Evidence:
    ☐ Determine whether the runner can supply live rendering, captured frames, component-tree snapshots, or only textual state.
    ☐ Document supported evidence modes and their availability conditions.
    ☐ Create Host Evidence from Panel, an evidence viewport, Property List, Status Badge, TooltipLabel where contextual help is required, and supported overlay treatments.
    ☐ Show the evidence supplied for the selected trace step.
    ☐ When supplied, show focus path, active viewscreen, pointer position, input target, mounted-screen ownership, and selected widget bounds.
    ☐ Define unavailable, pending, stale, capture-failed, and host-disconnected states.
    ☐ Never label inferred state as captured or visually verified evidence.
    ☐ Do not make Host Evidence capture, infer, or retain runtime state.
    ☐ Do not imply full time travel unless the runner retains the required per-step state.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Host Evidence and its states.

3.7 DwarfSpec component: Failure Inspector:
    ☐ Create Failure Inspector from Panel, issue, Status Badge, Source Reference, Failure Comparison, WrappedLabel, code-frame, stack, and action components.
    ☐ Show failure category, concise message, source location, expected value, and actual value when available.
    ☐ Add a short code-frame treatment when source context is supplied.
    ☐ Keep the stack collapsed by default when concise failure context is sufficient.
    ☐ Provide Copy Failure, Copy Reproduce Command, and Open Source actions.
    ☐ Keep Open Source as the primary transition to VS Code.
    ☐ Keep behavior failure, infrastructure error, cleanup failure, and host-health evidence visually distinct.
    ☐ Show cleanup status only when cleanup failed, was interrupted, or could not be confirmed.
    ☐ Show quarantine or dirty-host state as a separate critical condition.
    ☐ Define assertion, Lua error, timeout, discovery error, cleanup failure, host loss, and cancellation states.
    ☐ Keep failure classification and editor integration outside Failure Inspector.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Failure Inspector and its states.

3.8 DwarfSpec component: Run Summary:
    ☐ Create Run Summary from supplied run state, progress, result counts, and elapsed time.
    ☐ Define compact and full presentations for title-bar and footer use.
    ☐ Compose Status Indicator or Status Badge instead of duplicating status artwork.
    ☐ Define idle, discovering, running, stopping, complete, cancelled, failed, and disconnected states.
    ☐ Keep result aggregation and progress calculation outside Run Summary.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Run Summary and its states.

3.9 DwarfSpec component: Reproduce Command:
    ☐ Create Reproduce Command from EditField or an appropriate read-only field presentation plus existing action components.
    ☐ Show one contextual command for the selected result or failure.
    ☐ Provide a Copy action.
    ☐ Add Open in Terminal only if the product supports it.
    ☐ Keep command generation, clipboard behavior, and terminal launching outside the component.
    ☐ Do not present Reproduce Command as a replacement shell or source editor.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Reproduce Command and its states.

3.10 DwarfSpec component: Debug Options:
    ☐ Create Debug Options from Panel, ConfigureButton, CycleHotkeyLabel, ToggleHotkeyLabel or RadioButton, Command Menu, Status Indicator, and targeting_prompt where applicable.
    ☐ Use Dropdown Control only when a debug choice set is too large or unclear to cycle.
    ☐ Include Break on Assertion only if supported by the runner.
    ☐ Include Break on Lua Error only if supported by the runner.
    ☐ Include Break on Cleanup Failure only if supported by the runner.
    ☐ Include Pause Before Input only if supported by the runner.
    ☐ Clearly indicate armed debug behavior without occupying the normal evidence pane.
    ☐ Use HelpButton for command help only if the debug-options composition exposes a supported help target.
    ☐ Keep variables, watches, general breakpoint management, and embedded source editing out of Debug Options.
    ☐ Keep debug execution and breakpoint behavior outside the component.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Debug Options and its states.

Phase 4: Compose the DwarfSpec runner experience
    ☐ Exit with every reusable screen panel, workspace layout, and representative viewer state composed from linked component instances.

4.1 Reusable screen panel: Runner Header:
    ☐ Create Runner Header from Panel, product title, Run Summary, compact TextButton actions, CycleHotkeyLabel or Dropdown Control configuration controls, optional ConfigureButton and HelpButton actions, and connection Status Badge.
    ☐ Provide Run Scope, Rerun Selected, Rerun Failed, Debug Selected, and Stop actions.
    ☐ Define enabled, disabled, focused, running, and stopping action states.
    ☐ Show current scope, run state, progress, elapsed time, and active test without decorative workflow copy.
    ☐ Use CycleHotkeyLabel for configurable Mode and Repeat values when each has a small finite option set.
    ☐ Use Dropdown Control for Mode or Repeat only when exposing the complete option set in a popup is more usable.
    ☐ Do not include reproduce-command editing or permanent debug configuration.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Runner Header and its states.

4.2 Reusable screen panel: Test Explorer Panel:
    ☐ Create Test Explorer Panel from Panel, Tree View With Columns, EditField, CycleHotkeyLabel or Dropdown Control, Status Indicator, Divider, and active-scope text.
    ☐ Show file, suite, and test hierarchy.
    ☐ Show one supplied, right-aligned aggregate status per visible tree item.
    ☐ Add search or text filtering.
    ☐ Add status filtering for failed, errored, flaky, cleanup-failed, and not-run tests.
    ☐ Evaluate tag and category filters against actual discovery metadata.
    ☐ Use FilteredList for any flat auxiliary filter-choice list instead of rebuilding its List, EditField, and no-match behavior.
    ☐ Do not replace the hierarchical Tree View With Columns with FilteredList.
    ☐ Define empty, loading, no-match, discovery-error, and disconnected states.
    ☐ Keep discovery, filtering, selection, and status aggregation outside the panel.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Test Explorer Panel and its states.

4.3 Reusable screen panel: Test Results Panel:
    ☐ Create Test Results Panel from Panel, Divider, its pane title, Breadcrumb, selection context, Run Summary, Generic Table View, and Attempt Selector.
    ☐ Keep TEST RESULTS visible as the pane title.
    ☐ Show the selected scope in Breadcrumb.
    ☐ Show the selected test name and active attempt in a separate context region.
    ☐ Preserve original runner ordering as the table's default no-sort mode.
    ☐ Keep none, ascending, and descending sorting for applicable columns.
    ☐ Reevaluate whether KIND provides more triage value than attempt outcome or failure category.
    ☐ Show status, duration, and concise anomaly detail without padded text.
    ☐ Make source locations actionable with Source Reference.
    ☐ Define empty, loading, running, complete, failed, filtered-empty, and disconnected states.
    ☐ Keep result discovery, sorting, selection, and attempt synchronization outside the panel.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Test Results Panel and its states.

4.4 Reusable screen panel: Command Trace Panel:
    ☐ Create Command Trace Panel from Panel, Divider, a pane title, selected-test and attempt context, Command Trace, and optional trace controls.
    ☐ Make Command Trace more prominent than debug configuration.
    ☐ Keep chronological traces unsortable.
    ☐ Keep the selected step unambiguous.
    ☐ Define empty, loading, streaming, complete, failed, and unavailable states.
    ☐ Keep cross-panel synchronization outside the panel.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Command Trace Panel and its states.

4.5 Reusable screen panel: Evidence Inspector:
    ☐ Create Evidence Inspector from Panel, Failure Inspector, Host Evidence, Property List, and Pages.
    ☐ Use TabBar with Pages only if Failure and Host Evidence require explicit selectable views.
    ☐ Keep both evidence types visible without tabs when the available space and state make that clearer.
    ☐ Prioritize selected-step evidence and concise failure context.
    ☐ Keep permanent breakpoint controls out of the panel.
    ☐ Define no-selection, normal-step, failed-step, cleanup-failure, unavailable-evidence, and disconnected states.
    ☐ Keep attempt selection, trace selection, capture, and failure classification outside the panel.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Evidence Inspector and its states.

4.6 Reusable screen panel: Output Drawer:
    ☐ Create Output Drawer from Panel or ResizingPanel, TabBar, Pages, widgets.List, Log Row, ToggleHotkeyLabel or RadioButton, CycleHotkeyLabel or Dropdown Control, Divider, and the normal Scrollbar.
    ☐ Retain DFHACK OUTPUT for raw console output.
    ☐ Add HOST EVENTS only if connection, focus, executor, queue, and quarantine events can be separated reliably.
    ☐ Retain REPORT only when exposing the raw structured report helps diagnose runner behavior.
    ☐ Do not duplicate the structured Command Trace as another raw-output tab.
    ☐ Add Follow Output, Wrap, and Filter controls.
    ☐ Use ToggleHotkeyLabel for labeled Follow Output and Wrap controls.
    ☐ Use RadioButton instead when the compact graphical binary-control presentation is more appropriate.
    ☐ Use CycleHotkeyLabel for small finite filter modes and Dropdown Control for larger filter choice sets.
    ☐ Define selection and highlighting for a raw-log entry correlated with the selected trace step.
    ☐ Define buffering, truncation, long-line, wrapping, and large-output behavior.
    ☐ Define collapsed, expanded, empty, streaming, paused-follow, filtered-empty, and disconnected states.
    ☐ Keep filtering, buffering, correlation, and stream ownership outside the drawer.
    ☐ Create a dedicated component page for Output Drawer and its states.

4.7 Reusable screen panel: Runner Status Bar:
    ☐ Create Runner Status Bar from Panel, Divider, Run Summary, and supplied host-health Status Badges.
